    description: "INV-linub L3, residual shape 1 of 2. PR #595 recovered an external receiver's\nmodule path for the shapes where the type reaches _var_types as a plain\nidentifier binding. A STRUCT-FIELD receiver does not: `h.f.write_all(...)`\nloses the type upstream of normalization, in the receiver walk, because the\nreceiver node is a field_expression rather than an identifier and\n_var_types is keyed by variable name.\n\nStrategy 1.5 already resolves self.field receivers through\nanalyzer.resolve_receiver_type + field_type_registry -- but only to bind a\nFIRST-PARTY target symbol. When the field's type is external the lookup misses,\n`resolved` stays False, and the same drop happens: the type was computed and\ndiscarded. So the machinery exists and the fix is likely to be a narrow reuse\nof it on the unresolved-external branch, mirroring what PR #595 did for\n_var_types.\n\nMEASURED, 11 rust repos, post-#595 tree: `write_all` matches 5 catalogued\nmethod sites and MISSES 125. `lock` is the single largest miss in the cohort at\n440. Both are dominated by field and chained receivers.\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS MORE THAN #595 DID: #595 moved io-boundaries (303 -> 360\nclassified rust call sites) but moved ZERO taint flows, because the rows it\nunlocked were host_info_read and fs_read -- and fs_read is excluded from\nAUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP by design. `write_all` is fs_write, which IS an\nauto-derived SINK. This shape is on the sink side, so unlike #595 it can\nactually change a verdict.\n\nMEASURE THROUGH L4 ON FINDINGS, per INV-linub's standing rule, not on edges.\nTripwire available: 98/1206 catalogued trait-method sites matched today."  # d302
    fields: {}  # d302
